  "dataCard": {},
  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/benitochromis-riomuniensis-2/",
      "html": "<p>Benitochromis are closely related to the Pelvicachromis species and can be compared quite well with these peaceful and colorful cichlids regarding aquarium biology.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/benitochromis-riomuniensis-2/",
      "html": "<p>This species originates from Cameroon.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/benitochromis-riomuniensis-2/",
      "html": "<p>In contrast to Pelvicachromis, which are cave brooders, Benitochromis are biparental, ovophilous mouthbrooders. This means that both parents take care of eggs and fry; eggs and fry are passed from parent to parent several times per day. The eggs are taken into the mouth immediately after spawning (= ovophilous).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/benitochromis-riomuniensis-2/",
      "html": "<p>There is no common name for this species so far; the fish shown are German-bred. Due to the pretty yellow lips and throat, the name \"Golden Throated King Cichlid\" is suggested.</p>"
    }
  ],
